  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 Days...\n\nToday looks similar to yesterday with a few notable differences.\nThe first of which is that there is a much higher concentration \nof wildfire smoke over the region, esepcailly northern parts of \nthe CWA. The ASOS at KMLS is even reporting some visibility \nreductions as of the latest observation. This smoke will aid in \nkeeping temps even cooler than previously forecast. Due to this I \nlowered high temperatures today by a few degrees region wide. The \nEastern Montana Plains should still be able to break the triple \ndigit mark but this is virtually out of the question now for \nBillings.\n\nWe are watching another shortwave move into the region this\nafternoon which will bring another chance for storms. Today's\nshortwave is expected to be a touch farther south than \nyesterday's feature. Inverted-v style soundings support another \nday of gusty winds being the main threat, but now with \ntemperatures cooler than originally expected, this may diminish \nthe threat by keeping the low-level lapse rates not as steep. PWAT\nvalues are around 1.1\" near Sheridan which could allow for some \nisolated heavy rainfall in a similar fashion to what we saw with \nthe stronger storms yesterday.\n\nThe unfortunate news is that there is really no progressive flow \naloft through the entire work week and thus the ridge shows little\nto no signs of changing for several days. Highs in the 90s to \n100s appear likely to stick around into for the remainder of July.\nThis could pull some monsoonal moisture up into the region with \nthe chance for some showers and storms each day, particularly \nnear the mountains however coverage will probably be low any \ngiven day with a low chance for wetting rain. Additionally,\nwildfire smoke from the several complexes in Eastern OR will also\ncontinue to be around the area due to the predominately westerly \nflow. \n\nModels are now showing an increase in moisture over the eastern \n1/4 of the CWA into the middle of the week which could turn into a\nheavy rain and marginal severe weather risk Tuesday through \nThursday. This will need to be monitored as wildfire smoke, the \nstrength of the monsoonal flow, and just how much surface moisture\nis advected out of the plains are all variables that we still \nhave questions around. \n\nThere continues to be a signal that a ridge crasher event may\nimpact our area next weekend which would usher in cooler temps \nbehind it. This type of event would also bring the potential for \ngusty winds and maybe some storms as well which would be a\npotential concern for fire weather after a prolonged stretch of\nhot and quasi-dry conditions. WMR",
